## Issues

- [ ] `description` is ~870 chars (cap 200) — packs four contract paragraphs
  (parseBrainAide as source of truth, null-args structural early-return,
  byte-for-byte drift comparison, four-state vocabulary, ordering precedence,
  no-stat invariant). Trim to "structural classifier for brain wiring state,
  returns a closed four-state union, never mutates."
  Traces to: `description` field | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (function names, type signatures)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `intent` is ~334 words (cap 100) and restates the description with
  added paragraphs about precedence ordering, no-registry-import,
  no-name-branching, no-re-implementation-of-parsing, no-pattern-matching-for-sentinels.
  Most of this is Strategy.
  Traces to: `intent` field | Brevity Contract caps
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 18 items (cap 4) — by far the worst desired-list
  count in the tree. Each item is paragraph length encoding code-level
  invariants: `interpolateArgs` is unconditional, structural equality not byte
  equality, no-throws guarantee, single-positional `(root: string)` signature,
  shared `BrainState` type module location, `hints` populated unconditionally,
  hints shape and origin unchanged. Almost every item fails the outcomes-purpose
  test — type checker / unit test catches all of these.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired` | Brevity Contract caps + outcomes-purpose test (most items are code-level invariants)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.undesired` has ~16 items, all paragraph length, each enumerating
  a retired regression class by exact identifier name (`resolveBackend`,
  `getDriverById`, `brainBackends`, retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` sentinel, retired
  `invalid-path` state, byte-equality drift, etc.). Forbidden content (retired
  prior designs, migration history).
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Strategy` has 11 paragraph-length decisions (cap ~5). Most encode
  control-flow ordering ("parse → null-check → compare"), the exact precedence
  rule, the no-path-validation rule, the structural-vs-byte equality rule,
  the unconditional-interpolation rule, the name-as-metadata rule. These are
  plan/code-level decisions.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Brevity Contract caps
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `## Bad examples` reproduces ~10 TypeScript code snippets verbatim,
  showing the exact pattern the contract forbids. Per the Brevity Contract,
  function bodies, type signatures, and exact strings are forbidden in `.aide`.
  These code snippets are the textbook anti-pattern of bloat — the spec is
  doubling as a code-style document. Compress to brief prose descriptions
  of each anti-pattern.
  Traces to: `## Bad examples` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (TypeScript code, function signatures)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Good examples` reproduces full JSON `BrainState` returns with exact
  brand strings, exact paths, exact field counts. Per the contract, examples
  should be brief and pattern-illustrative.
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Forbidden content (exact strings, JSON shapes)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] Sibling redundancy with the project root and `src/.aide`: the four-state
  vocabulary, no-stat-invariant, source-of-truth-direction, no-auto-repair-on-drift,
  YAML-null-as-unwired-slot signal, parser-as-canonical-reader rule are all
  restated verbatim from parents. Most of `outcomes.undesired` is "don't do X"
  rules where X is already forbidden by ancestors.
  Traces to: outcomes, Strategy, Bad examples | Sibling-redundancy
  Misalignment: spec-gap
  Category: Sibling-redundancy

## Retro

The buildBrainState spec is the canonical example of a code-level invariant
list masquerading as outcomes. 18 desired bullets is structurally impossible
to defend as domain success criteria — domain success has 3-4 things you'd
ship a release on; everything beyond that is implementation invariant.

The Bad examples reproduce TypeScript anti-patterns verbatim. The defense
against `args.some(a => a.includes("<BRAIN_PATH>"))` is a unit test asserting
that pattern is absent, not a Bad example with the snippet inline. The
spec should commit to "detection is structural via JS `=== null`, never
pattern-matching string contents" and let a unit test pin the rule.

After the brain-layer parent is introduced and rooted, this spec shrinks to:
"structural classifier for brain wiring state; returns the closed
four-state union; reads brain.aide first; null in args short-circuits to
no-mcp-entry." Most of the 18 outcomes collapse into invariants that already
live at the parent.
